#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""One Make iteration: build, print gates, motion, comparison images, review.

    make_round <project>/cad [--component part_<role>.step.py]
               [--require-component-passes] [--ref LABEL=PATH[@AZ,EL[,TOL]] ...]
               [--nozzle 0.4] [--overhang-angle 45] [--all-parts] [--no-motion]
               [--full] [--out DIR] [--json] [--record-visual FEEDBACK.json]
               [--record-review REVIEW.json] [--record-unlock UNLOCK.json]
               [--shared-helpers] [--interface ID]
               [--report-blocked BLOCKED.json] [--clear-blocked ANSWER.json]
               [--blocked-reports] [--propose-amendment PROPOSAL.json]
               [--record-amendment-review REVIEW.json] [--contract-amendments]
               [--worker-nonce HEX] [--self-check]

Every verdict comes from the original cad tools, run unchanged; this script
only sequences them, remembers what changed between rounds, and prints a
summary short enough to read instead of the logs. It never lowers a threshold
or edits source. No silhouette score is computed: every reference is composed
beside the model, rendered at the camera the reference declares, and a reader
compares their form (ADR 0076).

An assembly round shows every reference the Wish sealed, unless a current
component round already showed it; it never passes on an empty reference list
when the Wish has references. In Contract Mode a component round shows the
sealed image of its own Unique Geometry, and the assembly never shows that
image against the whole object.

A component round passes on build, print gates and an independent reviewer's
recorded agreement (--record-review). The round policy (ADR 0081) makes the
build -> review -> repair loop mandatory: a round that passed its checks is
reviewed before the Component's geometry may change again; a shape round is
the first geometry-changing round after a disagreeing review, and after
SHAPE_REPAIR_LIMIT of them a disagreeing review is recorded as a component
acceptance the run reports when it ends. An agreeing review or an acceptance
locks the Component until a Shared Helper it imports changes or the Workshop
Manager records an assembly unlock (--record-unlock); a rerun that rebuilds
the reviewed B-rep carries the review forward. A round that fails its checks
is not rendered. A component packet binds only the Component's own files and
the Shared Helpers it imports. The assembly round keeps the Workshop
Manager's --record-visual, the blind review and --full.
In a run with the make_round guard (ADR 0080) only a Component Worker runs a
component round: the guard hook passes --worker-nonce, the round records it,
and the host refuses a component round whose nonce it did not issue.

When the sealed Design Contract has an Interfaces section (ADR 0082), the
Workshop Manager first runs --shared-helpers: it builds the samples under
samples/ and runs the print gates on them, and a pass freezes the Shared
Helpers by hash. Component rounds refuse to start before that freeze and
report every frozen helper they import that has changed since, with the
Components that import it. A component round of a separable Interface also
checks its Keep-out Envelope. --interface ID runs the coupled motion check of
one Coupled Interface on its locked Components; a failure unlocks the
contract's yielding Component, and assembly rounds with
--require-component-passes need a current passing check of every Coupled
Interface. Only the Workshop Manager runs those two modes. An Interface may
name one instance of a geometry, <id>#<n>; the Component's
assembly_pose(shape, pose, instance) places it, and the Component is what
locks, stales and unlocks.

A schema 3 Design Contract gives every reference its Reference Camera (ADR
0083). A component round then composes each reference beside the Component
in its Display Pose, assembly_pose(shape, None), rendered at that camera;
front, top and iso stay in the print stance. Every Component of such a
contract defines assembly_pose, and a round of one that does not is refused
before anything is built. Its reviewer may answer camera mismatch instead of
agreeing or disagreeing: that is not a Shape Round, the worker gets no repair
text, and the run waits until the host amends that one reference's camera
(the run root's CONTRACT-AMENDMENTS.json, written only by the host).

A schema 4 Design Contract gives every Interface its text (ADR 0084). A
component round then writes the Component's own contract rows and the text
of every Interface naming it into its visual packet and its summary, so the
reviewer and the worker read the sealed contract rather than a copy. A
review may list reference_conflicts beside its differences: places where the
reference shows what the contract forbids. The Design Contract wins: a
conflict is not a difference, costs no Shape Round, never reaches the
worker, and is reported when the run ends.
A Component Worker that cannot proceed -- two contract rows that cannot
both hold, or a choice only the Workshop Manager may make -- records a
Blocked Report (--report-blocked, issue #88) quoting the rows verbatim, in
measure/blocked-reports.jsonl beside its rounds. The Manager answers it
(--clear-blocked) with a decision, a decision waiting on another Component,
or a need quoting the rows. A waiting report stays open until that Component
passes a round; --blocked-reports then marks it woken. While any report is
open an assembly round and --full refuse to run, and the host refuses the
Make proposal and Make acceptance.
A decided ruling binds the Component Reviewer (issue #97): every later
packet of that Component carries its rulings (report, rows, request,
ruling), and a carried review needs the same rulings. A reviewer who thinks
a ruling wrong lists it under ruling_disputes, not differences: a dispute
costs no Shape Round, never reaches the worker, and a review whose only
findings are disputes agrees. A review of a packet older than a ruling is
refused; an unchanged rerun gets a fresh packet without a Shape Round.
Where a Contract Contradiction's smallest fix changes nothing a sealed
reference shows, the Manager may propose a Contract Amendment instead of a
need (--propose-amendment, ADR 0085). A fresh contract-reviewer reads its
packet and every sealed reference; --record-amendment-review applies it only
when the reviewer confirms the rows cannot both hold, the change is the
smallest and no reference shows it. Rounds then read the amended rows, and a
Component whose rows changed unlocks. While one awaits review an assembly
round and --full refuse to run.
The owner may amend the contract on resume (`workshop resume --amend-contract`,
issue #100): the host writes an Owner Contract Amendment into the run root's
CONTRACT-AMENDMENTS.json. Rounds read its rows after every applied in-run
amendment, a Component whose own rows it changed unlocks, and from then on
--propose-amendment is refused: a further contradiction stops on a need.
This tool makes no model calls.

Exit 0: all checks and the recorded (or carried) review passed. Exit 1:
failed or pending. Exit 2: the round could not run or the policy refused it.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import hashlib
from importlib.metadata import PackageNotFoundError, version as package_version
import json
import math
import os
import re
import signal
import runpy
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
from pathlib import Path

STATE_NAME = "make-round-state.json"
